Both findings are fixed. Summary of what changed:

## BLOCKER — the two VMs disagreed on `break`/`skip` in a block with its own frame

`src/bytecode/vm.rs` now tracks **lexical loop ownership** instead of looking only at the frame the instruction runs in:

- **`LoopOwner` + `Frame::loop_owner`** — a frame records the loop it was written inside, read from the instruction that entered it (`TEST`, `DEF_OBJECT`, or the `TRY` a `catch`/`finally` records). `loop_at` falls back to it when the frame has no loop of its own, so all four shapes (`catch`, `finally`, `test`, `object`) now name the loop around them. **Call frames record nothing**, keeping the function-body refusal the tree VM draws (`src/bytecode/vm.rs:1596`).
- **`unwind_frames_above`** finishes the frames between the instruction and the loop — loops unwound, handlers/scopes/stack released, an `object` body registering its type (`src/bytecode/vm.rs:1830`).
- Four ordering rules the fix forced, each pinned by a corpus program:
  - an interrupted `finally` in a crossed frame is still owed (`abandon_handlers` counts handlers above the owning frame);
  - frames are finished **before** the loop entry is created — otherwise a `while`'s first-turn `break` was a no-op (`while` + `test` + `break` printed 2 instead of 1);
  - the loop's variable stays bound while the `finally` runs (`put_back_binding` / `deferred_bindings`), because the turn hasn't ended yet;
  - `Exit::Left` is *read*, not taken, by each nested driver, so the caller owning the region is the one told.

## MINOR — the refusal was unspecified

`SPEC.md` "Break and Skip" and `docs/GRAMMAR.md` §4.4 now state the refusal and message, that it is a runtime error `try … catch error` catches, the two placements refused for the same reason (function body; `object` body outside any loop), and the two rules above.

## Coverage

13 new corpus programs (differential, both VMs), 9 new `test` blocks in `tests/test_loop_control.rb`, 6 new Rust tests, plus two renamed corpus keys that stated the old no-op. Reverting the one line the fix adds turns **14 of 368** corpus programs red.

## Gates (all re-run by me)

| Gate | Result |
|---|---|
| `cargo fmt --all -- --check` | pass, no diff |
| `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` | pass, zero warnings |
| `cargo test --all-targets` | 532 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored (24 binaries) |
| `rb test` | 269 run, 269 passed; `examples/*.rb` 6/6, `modules/*.rb` 2/2 exit 0 |

`./rbops/verify.sh` is still absent from this checkout (FINDINGS §1). FINDINGS §6 is closed, §7 is closed, and a **new pre-existing** panic found while probing — an `object` declared inside an `object` crashes the bytecode VM on HEAD too — is recorded as open §8 rather than folded into this round.
